tice that $f(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =({e}^{-x}-{e}^{-(-x)}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x}-{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-e}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-e}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-e}^{0}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ A+{\\int }_{0}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0059f2c8a1ff9b039f940d3a670f35b9ed8de709180d764dcd7bc7aa4af023"} {"question" "q-156191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9c1ec128486d1779cde54681fee1a120288070b2589fe0b7e6859e2636d19e74"} {"question" "q-156191" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0059f2c8a1ff9b039f940d3a670f35b9ed8de709180d764dcd7bc7aa4af023"} {"question" "q-156191" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-A"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}A"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "A"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|A|"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0059f2c8a1ff9b039f940d3a670f35b9ed8de709180d764dcd7bc7aa4af023"} {"question" "q-156191" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-A"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0059f2c8a1ff9b039f940d3a670f35b9ed8de709180d764dcd7bc7aa4af023"} {"question" "q-334359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0059f2c8a1ff9b039f940d3a670f35b9ed8de709180d764dcd7bc7aa4af023"} {"question" "q-334359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\sqrt[5]{(-x)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[5]{-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e0059f2c8a1ff9b039f940d3a670f35b9ed8de709180d764dcd7bc7aa4af023"} {"question" "q-334359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9c1ec12